{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-region-mapping.md", "content": "# Compact region-to-output mapping

When a partitioned input has a much smaller output, investigate a region summary as a live task class instead of assuming a same-size edit or stamp. Identify separator rows/columns, compute the number and arrangement of regions, and associate each region with the corresponding output cell. Inspect marker presence, marker color/identity, count, and position within each region as candidate encodings. Verify the exact output—including default values for empty regions—against every demonstration before transfer. Output dimensions alone do not establish the mapping. Before spending another demo request, list at least two candidate encodings and state what observation in the next pair would distinguish them.

In a demo with sparse markers embedded in a regular partitioned canvas, the compact output preserved a 6×3 region arrangement and represented localized marker colors at corresponding output positions; most empty positions retained the canvas color. Treat this as a diagnostic pattern, not a universal rule: confirm separators and correspondence in the current task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-4/play/users/u074/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-region-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/periodic-field-cross-demo-test.md", "content": "# Periodic-field hypothesis test across heterogeneous demos

When a demo output is a dense repeating field but other examples look unlike it, neither force a shared rule from visual similarity nor dismiss the periodic case as unrelated. Treat the pattern as a testable abstraction: record the output's row/column period, color cycle ordering, direction, and phase/origin; compare these with the input's sparse diagonal tracks, marker sequence, or stripe ordering. Distinguish period from phase (a correct cycle with wrong origin still fails exact replay).

Replay the proposed generator against every complete demo. Include cases where the output is uniform or striped: these may be degenerate instances of a shared periodic generator, or evidence against it, and must not be ignored. A test input that visually resembles a periodic encoding only motivates the hypothesis; it does not establish mapping. If no deterministic cycle/phase rule reproduces all outputs, label it unresolved rather than submitting cosmetic rotations/reversals or repeatedly requesting demos without a discriminating prediction.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-4/play/users/u074/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/periodic-field-cross-demo-test.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md", "content": "# Distinguish visual heterogeneity from task-rule drift

A fresh stream used demos with very different dimensions, palettes, backgrounds, and motifs from the test. The demos' observed edits were themselves disparate localized edits: demo 1 changed a pair of 5s to 6s; demo 2 changed a single 8 to 9; demo 3 changed a 7-cell to 9; demo 4 changed a local 7 region to 8; demo 5 changed two 7s to 3; demo 6 recolored a compact 2x3 patch from 2 to 5. No shared selector or abstract transformation was derived, and repeated requests were made without analyzing a cumulative delta ledger. The session's final candidate merely recolored the test's 8 structures to 4 without support from the demos.

Reusable inference lesson: neither assume that different-looking demos are unrelated nor force a common rule based on colors/salience. Precisely record each pair's delta, object context, and unchanged candidates; then seek measurable invariant relations (shape, relative position, local neighborhood, object role) across examples. Test any proposed rule by exact full-output replay on every demo. If deltas remain unlinked, further demos require a specific competing-hypothesis prediction; serial requests do not resolve uncertainty by themselves. For the test, keep its actual colors and structures unchanged unless a validated rule predicts edits.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-4/play/users/u074/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/stream-expansion-and-failed-stamp.md", "content": "# Stream expansion demos and representation checks

In a task with a 5×10 test, the first two demos had 1D inputs and expanded outputs: 9×1 → 27×2, with each input value repeated across each output row and the sequence arranged as reversed/original/reversed; 1×15 → 3×30, with three identical rows and a sequence arranged as reversed/original. These establish that 1D-to-2D expansion depends on orientation, but do not establish a mapping for a genuinely 2D test.

A speculative answer incorrectly treated the 5×10 test as a 3×2 stamp replication over groups of three rows. The key failure was unsupported transfer of a construction despite the test not matching either demonstrated input class. After the incorrect response, the correct next step is to reopen output representation and seek evidence that distinguishes candidate 2D mappings, rather than cosmetically modifying the stamp. For demos, articulate what the new pair would disambiguate before paying the request cost; requests should resolve a concrete uncertainty.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-4/play/users/u074/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/stream-expansion-and-failed-stamp.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/task-29b1b5eb-edge-extension.md", "content": "# Edge-extension evidence from a heterogeneous ARC stream

In a stream instance with varied palettes and grid sizes, demonstrations consistently modified only cells immediately to the right of an existing horizontal same-color run, recoloring the formerly background suffix to color 1. Examples included short left-edge runs extended to the right boundary and right-edge-adjacent bars in different locations; complete bars remained unchanged. A test with sparse/colorful motifs still followed this family: only the end of the horizontal run at the right edge was converted, leaving the rest intact.

Process lesson: do not spend the full demo budget automatically. Extract each pair's exact changed coordinates, compare with all unchanged candidate runs, and formulate a measurable selector (e.g., a bar ending short of the right boundary versus full-width bars) before another request. Even when a rule is evident, validate the predicted row and exact suffix length against every demo. Keep the session-specific rule as evidence, not as a universal task-ID mapping.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-4/play/users/u074/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/task-29b1b5eb-edge-extension.md"}
